  ],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "English uses do both for performing an action and as an empty helper in questions and negatives. Many languages have no such helper and form questions by word order or a small particle, so auxiliary do has no direct translation.",
    "grammar": "In the simple present and past, ordinary verbs form questions and negatives with do, does, or did plus the base form: does she like it, they did not go. The main verb do can take this helper too: what do you do.",
    "culture": null,
